s particularly women and  girls  across across our country and that's  something that really concerns us um and  we we know uh particularly in the last  few months and some of the events some  of the tragic deaths of of women last  year have really really changed um how  some communities particularly women and  girls feel about policing so yes we hear  that trust and confidence  and maggie i mean  one of the good news is is that you've  been appointed the lead in the police to  actually tackle this across police  forces so uh priti patel the home  secretary said to me that she wanted to  make it a national priority and she's  put you in place which means it has  become a national priority which is a  positive thing but i guess the thing is  is that  what follows through from that and one  of the questions i had for you  is that  does your appointment does the fact the  government's making this a national  priority mean that in practice the  government now might hit these rate  review targets that they set  and that was to get to nearly five over  five thousand prosecutions and nearly  three thousand convictions by 2024  that's going back to 2016. you'll know  all of this uh levels last year  prosecutions were 1500 just to put it in  some context can you give the commitment  today now that you think with you at the  lead and this prioritization that the  government can hit those targets and  women can have confidence by 2024 the  government will hit those targets what i  can talk about is i'm absolutely  committed to  ensuring that the things that need to  change do change within policing but we  also know  that we have to work with other sectors  with a range of other different  organizations including  the cps if we're really going to make a  difference for the criminal justice  system as a whole so i'm ambitious for  change and i know that we have a plan  and will make some uh significant  changes over the next year two years  moving forward but we we're talking  