d  girls it's on the level of of an  epidemic in terms of the information and  the data that we have  you outlined at the beginning we know  what the problem is so we we know that  it's it's an issue for society and  therefore what we see in society we will  also see in policing but we also know  that this is something that we have to  keep shining the light on and the more  that we do i think the more cases we're  going to see coming to light around our  own workforce around police officers and  staff  behaving in a way that falls below the  standard that we would expect within  policing and we will root that out and  already are on the way to do that  through the plans that we have because  mag actually you now you bring it up i  mean dozens of male police officers are  continuing to exploit their position and  take sexual advantage of women 66  officers and members of the police  you'll know this face disciplinary  proceedings for alleged abuse of  position for sexual  purpose have you witnessed any of these  attitudes among your colleagues  personally first of all just to go back  there there is an absolute plan to root  out any any behavior or conduct that  falls below what we would expect in  policing and the many officers and staff  that i work with and i have led over the  last few years want that we don't want  people in policing that uh bring down  the reputation of policing  and don't fit with the ethics and values  we hold  are so important so really important to  know that there's that plan in terms of  what what um i see i i yes i i  experience and see what women tell me um  they've faced within policing and and in  other professions working alongside  policing um and it's really important  that we have a culture where those  things are talked about amongst our  teams and our supervisors and women  concerns you've been a senior police  officer you you've  personally experienced sexism and i have  been really supported in my career  through policing i wouldn't be here i  wouldn't be doing the job i'm doing if i  didn't feel that policing is a fantastic  career and something to enjoy and be  part of that's really important i think  to get across i want women to come and  join policing i'm really pleased we've  got about 42 percent in our latest round  coming to join policing but yes i i've  seen sexism and i know that misogyny  exists in in police forces  and police the police service as a whole  and and really important that we tackle  that it exists because it exists in  society but i think there also is  perhaps something about being attracted  to power being attracted to  um  a culture that some  largely men may find find attractive and  want to come into um and uh because of  the co the ability to coerce and abuse  more people that are more vulnerable so  i think there's always that possibility  which we see in other professions as  well  and our vetting and and our culture has  to be one where  in teams people can have those  conversations and call out and feel that  they will be listened to if they feel so  you feel the more women in the police  force the more that dilutes that culture  that male culture i think it does it  absolutely dilutes it but also diversity  and and having men as we do have who  want to be  very strong allies for calling out  inappropriate culture and being part of  that change  just one quick question on  the most prominent police woman in the  country cressida dick um did you think  she was treated unfairly or was it  understandable she had to go how did you  feel about that as a fellow senior  policewoman the met commissioner is a  fantastic role model for for women in  policing um  her resignation really is a matter for  um the mayor of london and the home  secretary so i wouldn't comment but a  great role model for women in policing  so you found her inspiring absolutely  absolutely um  let's just tackle another problem i'm  actually running out i've got so many  questions um you talked about male  culture in the police but is the real  problem against with violence against  women and girls male culture in our  society has it got worse or are we  getting more vocal about not putting up  with it i i think very much the latter  i've in a working career of over 30  years and i've seen changes in in that  working career around our society but  there are attitudes we know still  prevail and i think a tipping point  which i have referred to others have  referred to as a watershed moment of  last year coming out of the pandemic the  spotlight that's been shown on what  happens behind closed doors and  sometimes in in public places to women  and girls and the everyone's invited  website that's set up by by girls and  young women um accounting for the things  that have happened to them i think women  and girls are saying enough we we don't  want to be treated like this we don't  have to have and put up with that type  of behavior and that is a moment in time  for me and my career for for me um as as  a woman in society and i think we need  to grab that moment  and to use what we know with that  information you started out with this  morning the epidemic in front of us we  need to be able to address it but  policing has to do that by working with  other  agencies with education with the rest of  the criminal justice system with with  health because ultimately this is i  think a public health issue around uh  violence and maggie obviously focus of  forces help  people speaking about it  helps but in practical terms what would  help you in terms of policing would it  help for example if misogyny became a  hate crime would it help if public  sexual harassment became a specific  offence i want to keep the spotlight on  violence against women and girls  i want there to be lasting change for  how women and girls feel safe in our  society i want women and girls to trust  policing and the plan we have i think  we'll get will go a long way to that but  i think it's a complex one misogyny is a  hate crime the government has accepted  the law commission advice that um it  won't be um included as a hate crime and  policing  enforces the laws we don't make it um i  think it's perhaps a very symbolic  gesture to have have done to make  misogyny hate crime but perhaps doesn't  really get to the root of the problem  that if we sexual harassment  well well i think the root of the  problem is behavior and attitudes of  boys and men we've got lots of offences  already  and we know that consistently we don't  use them as well as we should across our  43 police forces we don't use some of  the protective orders that we've got to  keep women and girls safe well enough so  let's get working on that and let's get  looking at the education and the  changing attitudes of boys and men  before we think we need more laws  because i think prevention and education  and tackling attitudes isn't necessarily  about creating new legislation  so i'm not entirely convinced but we  will work with government as they look  into  whether new laws are needed around  street-based harassment  and we will we will want to recognize  that there are additional pressures in  the 21st century online that might mean  new legislation well we are going to get  the online harms bill so well look  maggie i would like to carry on but i've  got to wrap that but thank you for  coming on and i hope you'll come back  again in a few months time and we could  look at some of the stats and see if  we're going the right way or the wrong  way thank you thank you so much

